  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through mid week.\n\nConditions will remain tranquil as high pressure persists over \nthe Mid-Atlantic through Wednesday before gradually moving \noffshore. Large scale subsidence and dry air lead to a \nprecipitation-free forecast with temperatures rising to several \ndegrees above normal as the high pulls offshore. This leads to \nhigh temperatures in the mid to upper 80s today, perhaps near \n90 Wednesday (slightly cooler in the mountains). Low \ntemperatures (and dew points) will generally be in the 50s and \n60s during this time. Some river valley fog is likely each \nmorning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return Thursday.\n\nThe upper-level ridge will begin to flatten out Wednesday before \nbreaking down and pushing south and east into Thursday. Several \npieces of upper-level energy rotating around an upper low \ncentered over the Hudson Bay will slowly push a cold front south\ntowards our region by Thursday. At the surface, high pressure \npushing offshore will bring increasingly warm and humid \nconditions to the area, with highs in the 80s to near 90 with \ndew points in the upper 60s to low 70s, with the highest values \nbeing Thursday.\n\nModel guidance over the past 24 hours has come into better agreement \nin regards to the progression of the front on Thursday, as well as \nthe upper-level pattern in association with it. The front \nreally looks like it will flatten out as it moves southward, and\nreally loses the upper-level support. The timing of peak \nheating and the highest shear just doesn't look to line up at \nthis point. There may be some jet-induced showers and storms \nlate Thursday as we move into the right entrance of the upper \njet centered over New England, but not necessarily expecting \nthose to be severe at that point, as the front should be largely\nthrough the region. At this point, the severe weather threat \nlooks very limited, but any storm that develops on the warm side\nof the boundary could bring some gusty winds along with it, but\nthe lack of a substantial wind field will really make that more\ndifficult with southward extent.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Cooler and less humid conditions are expected \non Friday, before humidity builds back in next weekend. \n\nHigh pressure will build to our north on Friday in the wake of \nThursday's frontal passage. Cooler and drier air will advect into \nthe region from the north in advance of the approaching high. Mostly \nsunny skies are expected, with high temperatures in the upper 70s \nand lower 80s. It will also feel considerably less humid, with \ndewpoints dropping into the upper 50s and lower 60s.\n\nHigh pressure will move offshore and a strong area of low pressure \nwill track across Hudson Bay on Saturday, causing winds to turn out \nof the south in response. Southerly winds will advect a more humid \nairmass back into the area, with dewpoints climbing into the upper \n60s and lower 70s. Chances for afternoon showers and thunderstorms \nwill accompany the more humid airmass. Both instability and shear \nvalues are forecast to remain low, so any storms that do form are \nexpected to stay on the weaker side.\n\nModel guidance begins to diverge a bit by Sunday and Monday, but \nmost solutions favor continued above average temperatures, with \nhighs in the mid to upper 80s. Ensemble guidance suggests that \nchances for afternoon showers and thunderstorms should trend \ndownward as Saturday's system tracks further east across Canada \nwithin zonal flow, but there are a few solutions (such as the 12z \nCanadian) that favor a more amplified longwave pattern and greater \nchances for showers and thunderstorms.",
